[Music]  every kid  knows what they want to be when they  grow up  when you're a kid you've got that candy  cane colored  anything is possible almost dream-like  view of the world  some kids want to be astronauts others  firefighters me  i was dramatic i wanted to be  an actress fantasized about it every day  never mind that i lacked the requisite  talent or parental support  this was my dream  and in that fantasy of fantasies that  deepest desire back when reality and  limits didn't exist  i was a supporting actress  a second fiddle to a lindsay lohan or a  hillary duff  i was never practicing any acceptance  speeches  deepest dreams tara was never up for any  awards  in my wildest most unattainable hopes  i was a sidekick  i've thought about this a lot in the  years since  i keep coming back to the sad  realization that as an ambitious child  with a huge imagination  i couldn't see myself as more than a  supporting player  in my own fantasy why couldn't i  well because the heroines in the tv  shows and movies that i watched growing  up weren't little brown girls  and without that model i couldn't even  dream of it  today i'm here to talk to you about how  deeply entertainment permeates our  culture  it impacts everything from what we think  about ourselves  to what we think about others and most  powerfully of all  it can actually impact what we do  it's an incredible tool and as a student  of public policy  i think it's time we used it for social  change  now i want to acknowledge that  entertainment has come a long way since  i was a kid  not just in terms of the representation  we see on screens  but also in terms of the kinds of  content we consume  for the purposes of today's talk i'm  defining entertainment as the content  that has  and continues to define the cultural  zeitgeist  film and television now first and  foremost  this entertainment impacts what we think  about ourselves  and what we believe we can achieve more  broadly the most prominent example of  this is known  as the scully effect let me take you  back  the year is 1996 and the x-files is one  of the most popular shows on television  if you didn't watch it it's kind of a  mystery criminal minds with aliens for  some reason it's a huge success  now the star of that show is jillian  anderson some of you may know her as  margaret thatcher on netflix's the crown  but back then she was dr dana scully  medical doctor hyper-competent scientist  and one of the first  prominent examples of a female scientist  on a major tv show  and guess what among women who watch the  show  dr scully's prominence can be directly  linked to an  increase in the desirability of stem  careers  a 43 percent increase to be exact  43 percent entertainment can  fundamentally impact what we believe we  can achieve  now how many women on boards have you  seen represented in entertainment  how many minority ceos it begs the  question  how much is entertainment reflecting the  sad realities of the world we live in  and how much is it perpetuating the  status quo  the scully effect shows us that  diversity and representation is  genuinely important not just because  what we see on screens  should reflect the world we live in but  because it can actually impact what we  believe we're capable of  second entertainment doesn't just impact  what we think about ourselves  it impacts what we think about others  now what we think about others is  directly tied to our empathy for others  and this is something progressive  politicians are very familiar with  they're trying to convince voters to  care about people that they might not  have a lot in common with  someone for example like an undocumented  immigrant  but politicians are failing to build  this empathy in the 30-second sound  bites they have our attention for  and even when we are listening our trust  in politicians is at a historic low  so if not from politicians where  can this empathy come from  well surprisingly entertainment may  provide a key  the organization define american studied  the television show superstore on nbc  it features a wide range of characters  including mateo  an undocumented immigrant  you grow to know and love him over the  course of the show  but eventually the narrative turns mateo  is taken by  ice for deportation  define american found that nearly half  of the people who watched this storyline  said they would take more pro-immigrant  related actions as a result  including advocating to family and  friends  that means the night this show aired  nearly 500  000 conversations across the u.s about  immigration took place  as a direct result of mateo's storyline  entertainment can fundamentally change  how we feel about other people  and lastly most powerfully of all  entertainment can actually impact what  we do  now one of the things we as americans do  or rather don't do  is register to donate our organs in the  event of a tragedy  there's over a hundred thousand  americans currently in need of a donated  organ  and yet less than half of the us  population is a registered organ donor  it's a politically sensitive issue  difficult to legally mandate  but without that requirement how can we  solve the issue  well should come as no surprise that  here again entertainment can provide a  key  the hollywood health and society  initiative at usc  did a study of four prominent tv shows  with organ donation story lines  csi new york numbers house and grey's  anatomy  they found viewers who are not  registered organ donors were more likely  to actively choose to become organ  donors  if the story line encouraged donation  that's a big deal and this change in  behavior isn't just  limited to organ donations  indian soap operas led to a reduction in  misogynistic preferences for sons  u.s medical dramas led to an increase in  breast cancer screening  and entertainment has even proven to  increase vaccination rates  so entertainment impacts what we think  about ourselves what we think about  others  and even what we do  it can even step in where public policy  makers are failing  but lastly i want to take a moment to  acknowledge what's at stake here  entertainment has the power to do a lot  of good  it also risks doing a lot of harm  the most infamous example of this is the  1915 film the birth of a nation  a racist film depicting the civil war  and reconstruction that directly led  to the resurgence of the kkk  there are more modern examples as well  color of change found that popular crime  shows directly led to a normalization of  racial injustice in this country  and the netflix show 13 reasons why has  been found to lead to a 30 percent spike  in teenage suicides following the  premiere  entertainment is beholden to the studios  and creatives who develop this content  there's a lot at stake if they get this  wrong  but they in turn are beholden to us  you and me the content consumers and how  we choose to spend our money  so what do i want you to take away from  this  well first i want you to recognize the  power entertainment has over  you whether you're a policy maker you're  in the industry or you just watch  netflix on a sunday night  i want you to ask yourself whether what  you're watching is reflecting the  realities of the world we live in  or better yet if it's pushing the  envelope and showing you a world to  aspire to  but most importantly of all i want you  to invest  in the power of entertainment for social  change  whether it's a direct investment in an  organization like color of change or  define american  or just an investment with the kinds of  films and tv  you choose to purchase and watch  entertainment is powerful we all want to  live in a world  with more women in stem more empathy for  immigrants more organ donors and yes  more brown girls on the big screen i  hope you'll join me in pushing towards  this goal  you

